1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Three questions about one algorithm: what is the cheapest run, the typical run, and the dearest run?

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Insertion sort on n keys: best n minus one, average n times n minus one over four plus n minus H of n, worst n times n minus one over two.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The average always lies between the best and the worst, whatever the distribution is. That much is free.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
GATE 2012 asked which relation always holds between the average and the worst. The answer was: the average is O of the worst.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Linear search over ten slots: one comparison if the key is first, ten if it is last, ten if it is absent.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
The worst case is a game: an adversary picks the input after reading your algorithm.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Saying the worst case is Theta of n is two claims: never more than c times n, and sometimes at least c prime times n.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
GATE 2024 Set 1 asked this of a single-pass sortedness check, and the answer was both O of N and Omega of N.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
An average is a weighted average over inputs, so it is not a number until the weights are named.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Key present with probability p at a uniform position: the average is p times n plus one over two, plus one minus p times n.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
At ten slots: p equal to one gives 5.5, p equal to a half gives 7.75, and p equal to 0.2 gives 9.1.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The best and the worst never moved. Only the distribution moved, and it carried the average most of the way to the worst.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Run first-element quicksort on all 40,320 orderings of eight keys and count the comparisons of each run.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The cheapest ordering costs 13 comparisons, the dearest 28, and the mean over all orderings is 16.92.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The closed form two times n plus one times H of n, minus four n, gives 16.9214 — the same number the enumeration produced.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The mean is not the middle. The median is 16 and the midpoint of the range is 20.5, but only the mean is the average case.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
Amortised analysis asks a different question: what does a whole sequence of operations cost?

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
A stack with MULTIPOP: one call can pop every item, so a single operation costs Theta of n.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
But each item is pushed once and popped once, so 102 operations cost 200 units in total.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
No probability was used. The aggregate method bounds the total, then divides by the number of operations.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A table that doubles when full: the first ten insertions cost 1, 2, 3, 1, 5, 1, 1, 1, 9 and 1.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
The 513th insertion copies 512 items, so one operation is Theta of n while the amortised cost is under two.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
1,024 insertions cost 2,047 units in total, so the amortised cost per insertion is just under 2.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Growth must be geometric. Growing by a fixed ten instead makes the cost per insertion rise without bound.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Accounting method: charge every insertion three units and keep the surplus in a bank.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The balance never goes negative, and the sum of the charges is an upper bound on the sum of the true costs.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Potential method: define a potential that is zero at the start and never negative, then charge the cost plus the change in potential.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
With potential two times items minus size, every insertion is charged at most three — the same answer by another route.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
The syllabus says worst-case time and space. They are two answers, and GATE asks for them separately.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Merge sort needs n extra cells and a stack of depth log n; quicksort needs no extra array but still has a stack.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
That stack is the space cost: depth 8 on eight sorted keys with a first-element pivot, and depth 4 when the splits balance.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
So quicksort is in place in the array sense and still Theta of n in the worst case in the stack sense.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
Randomised quicksort chooses its pivot by coin, so no input can be built to force the bad split.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The worst case is still n squared, and GATE 2001 asked exactly that: O of n squared.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Expected is a probability-weighted cost over the coins; amortised is a worst-case bound over a sequence.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The chance of hitting the all-bad run falls fast: 0.0032 at eight keys and 1.6 times ten to the minus nine at sixteen.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
First name the quantity: worst case, average case, amortised over a sequence, or expected over coins.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
If average, demand the distribution; if amortised, name the sequence and check that growth is geometric.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Then check the bound: O is an upper bound, Omega a lower bound, and a worst case usually deserves Theta.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
The four traps: average is not amortised, O is not the worst case, one cheap operation proves nothing, and space is separate.

